Looking at the code, there are two places where this pattern is used:
1. In the `fetchRepositoryDetails` function where they check `if (currentState is RepositoriesUiState.Success && results.isNotEmpty())`
2. There might be other places too.

The `update` function on `MutableStateFlow` is a convenient way to update the state based on the current state atomically. The signature is something like:
```kotlin
fun update(function: (T) -> T)
```

So instead of:
```kotlin
val currentState = _uiState.value
if (currentState is RepositoriesUiState.Success && results.isNotEmpty()) {
 val updatedRepositories = currentState.repositories.map { repo ->
 repo.fullName?.let { fullName ->
 results[fullName]?.let { starCount ->
 repo.copy(stargazersCount = starCount)
 } ?: repo
 } ?: repo
 }
 _uiState.value = RepositoriesUiState.Success(updatedRepositories)
}
```

They can do:
```kotlin
if (results.isNotEmpty()) {
 _uiState.update { currentState ->
 if (currentState is RepositoriesUiState.Success) {
 val updatedRepositories = currentState.repositories.map { repo ->
 repo.fullName?.let { fullName ->
 results[fullName]?.let { starCount ->
 repo.copy(stargazersCount = starCount)
 } ?: repo
 } ?: repo
 }
 RepositoriesUiState.Success(updatedRepositories)
 } else {
 currentState
 }
 }
}
```

